---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Icon from "@dst/ui/Icon.astro";
import { checkedOn, forum, forumDate } from "../content";
import { go } from "../outbound";
---
<Layout
  title="Future World Forum Dubai programme"
  description="What Future World Forum Dubai 2026 covers: fintech and crypto, AI and data, PropTech, urban cybersecurity, and smart infrastructure and mobility — and who each track is aimed at."
>
  <section class="container hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Programme</p>
    <h1>What the forum covers</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Future World Forum Dubai publishes its programme as five sectors rather
      than a timetable. One day, {forum.startTime}–{forum.endTime}, on
      {forumDate}. Below is each sector in the organiser's own framing,
      condensed, with what it means in practice for the people who show up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>The five sectors</h2>
    {forum.programme!.map((track) => (
      <article class="track">
        <h3>{track.heading}</h3>
        <p>{track.text}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>

  <Callout kind="note">
    <strong>No session times, speakers or exhibitor list have been published.</strong>
    As of {checkedOn} the sectors above are the whole of the published
    programme. A one-day event usually settles its running order six to eight
    weeks out; this page is updated when that appears.
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Who it is addressed to</h2>
    <p>
      The organiser names its intended audience explicitly, which is more
      useful than it sounds — it tells you which side of the room you would be
      in, and whether the answer is "buying" or "selling".
    </p>
    <ul class="tags">
      {forum.audience!.map((who) => <li>{who}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read together with the sectors, that is a room of public-sector buyers
      and technology vendors with investors along the edges. It is a
      business-development day more than a research one: nobody is presenting
      a paper, and the value on offer is who you meet in a city that actually
      deploys this category of system.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Getting a seat</h2>
    <p>
      Tickets run in a wide band and sales close well before the date — see
      <a href="/tickets/" title="Ticket prices, the deadline and the refund policy">the ticket page</a> for
      the figures, or go straight to the seller.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="button" href={go("tickets")} rel="nofollow sponsored" title="Ticket page for Future World Forum Dubai on Eventbrite">
        <Icon name="ticket" />
        Tickets on Eventbrite
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .track {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }
  .track h3 { margin: 0 0 var(--space-1); font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--accent); }
  .track p { margin: 0; max-width: 68ch; }
  .tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .tags li {
    padding: 0.3em 0.8em;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--surface);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
</style>
